{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch7EHHhPg3wLmh9bKh2Qk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<final_document>\nGood evening, commissioners. My name is [your name]. I've lived on Alder Road for 19 years, two blocks from St. Luke's Lutheran. I'm also a member of St. Luke's, and I wanted you to know that up front.\n\nI'm here to ask you to approve the permit for 64 affordable apartments on the church's back parking lot, with a traffic signal at Alder and 4th as a condition of approval.\n\nI'll start with traffic, because I know that's what many of my neighbors are worried about, and I understand why. Some mornings around 8, traffic on Alder already backs up past my house. I'm not going to tell you that 310 more trips a day won't matter.\n\nBut the traffic study answers that worry. Without changes, the intersection at Alder and 4th would drop from a C to a D at rush hour. Cascade Housing Partners has offered to pay for a signal there. With that signal, the study says the intersection stays at a C. So this project doesn't have to make things worse for those of us who live on Alder, as long as the signal actually gets built. That's why I'm asking you to make it a condition, not just leave it as an offer.\n\nOn parking, the plan has 72 spaces for 64 units, which is more than one space per apartment.\n\nNow, why this project matters. The county's own housing report says we are short about 2,100 affordable homes. Right now, 412 households are on the housing authority's waiting list, and the average wait is three years. Every one of these 64 apartments would rent to a household earning under 60 percent of the area median income. Those are exactly the families who are waiting.\n\nAnd the site makes sense. It's 1.8 acres of parking lot that sits empty six days a week. Nothing gets torn down and no open land gets paved. We'd be putting land that's mostly unused to good use.\n\nI've lived in this neighborhood for 19 years, and I believe it has room for 64 more households. That's especially true with a signal that keeps our busiest intersection at the rating it has today.\n\nPlease approve this permit, with the traffic signal at Alder and 4th as a condition. Thank you.\n</final_document>\n\n**Before tonight:**\n- **Length:** The speech is about 390 words, which runs a little under 3 minutes at a calm pace. Time yourself reading it out loud once. If you run long, cut the parking paragraph first.\n- **Check the signal offer:** If you can, make sure Cascade's offer to pay for the signal is in the staff report or the application. If a commissioner asks, you'll want to say where it's written down.\n- **What I didn't add:** You didn't know the permit type, so the speech just says \"the permit.\" I also didn't claim the parking will keep cars off Alder. The 72-for-64 figure supports that, but nothing you gave me proves it. If staff or the builder has said so on the record, you can add it."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":926,"cache_read_input_tokens":9911,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":926},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ebd7b3e7-3593-4ee0-9555-fe3454b7227c","uuid":"7ab15720-0bc0-4526-b6a5-b89d34a7bb17","timestamp":"2026-11-16T20:22:23.865Z","request_id":"req_011Ch7EHHYDGgAXUVx4jk6jp"}
